maya: Quick check — we're seeing duplicate side effects when our Go SDK retries streaming generate calls after a 429. Server sometimes streams a few tokens then connection is cut and our client retries the whole request, which re-triggers downstream processing. What do folks recommend: idempotency-key, resume tokens, or server-side dedupe? cc @jon

jon: Short answer: use idempotency keys for non-resumable side-effecting ops, and resume tokens for continuing streaming generation. 429 vs 5xx matters: 429 often has Retry-After so prefer backoff + wait; 5xx usually indicates transient infra and you should retry with exponential backoff + jitter.

liam: For streams specifically, if your server can emit a resume token (or final chunk id) that's best. Client flow: keep the last-acked chunk id locally, then call resume(model, prompt, resume_token). That avoids re-running side-effects.

maya: Our backend can't easily support resume tokens for the next few sprints. We _do_ have idempotency hooks for HTTP POSTs. Would adding an Idempotency-Key header to the streaming call help?

jon: It can, but caveats: Idempotency keys are effective only if the server stores the request result and can return a stored response instead of re-executing side-effects. For streaming, that means the server must be able to replay the same token stream or a pointer to it. If server just ignores duplicate keys and still executes, you won't avoid duplication.

jon: Implementation sketch for client backoff + idempotency:```
// generate with idempotency
req.Header.Set("Idempotency-Key", uuid)
// retry policy
for attempt := 0; attempt < max; attempt++ {
  resp, err := client.Do(req)
  if err == nil && resp.StatusCode == 200 { break }
  if resp != nil && resp.StatusCode == 429 {
    // if Retry-After provided, respect it
    wait := parseRetryAfter(resp)
    sleep(wait)
  } else {
    // exponential backoff with jitter
    sleep(base*2^attempt + rand.Float()*jitter)
  }
}
```

liam: Also watch for streaming semantics in each language client. Go's http.Transport may buffer differently than node's fetch; make sure you handle partial chunks and don't auto-commit downstream actions until you see a final token or an explicit "commit" control token.

maya: Good point. We're currently committing side-effects when we receive the first 'assistant' token to reduce latency. Sounds like we should delay commit until final chunk or an explicit "commit" control token, otherwise retries will duplicate.

jon: Exactly. Two practical options while server resume is pending: 1) keep idempotency-key + change client to buffer until commit; or 2) make the side-effect itself idempotent (e.g., write with unique op idempotency check). Combining both is safest.

maya: Got it. For the backoff knobs, any suggested defaults?

jon: Reasonable defaults we use in SDKs: base=200ms, max_backoff=10s, multiplier=2, attempts=6, jitter=uniform(0,0.3*wait). For 429 if Retry-After > 0, override backoff with that value but still apply jitter.

liam: And document language-specific gotchas: Go: Transport keep-alive + streaming read semantics. Node: stream decoding and backpressure. Python: requests vs httpx differences. File an internal doc note and maybe add a checklist to the SDK PR.

devex-bot: Shared doc: https://redwood.internal/devex/retries-idempotency — includes sample code and server checklist :thumbsup:

maya: Thanks — I'll change the client to buffer until commit and add Idempotency-Key as a short-term mitigation. I'll open a PR and ping for a quick review.

jon: Happy to review. If you want, add a tiny integration test that simulates a 429 mid-stream and verifies no duplicate side-effects.

maya: Will do. Thanks all :pray:
